{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem","id":1033969,"title":"Just how \"gothic\" is Requiem?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-18T05:47:30-08:00","id":1033969,"title":"Just how \"gothic\" is Requiem?","content":"We've been discussing the thematic differences between Requiem and Masquerade with my troupe lately, and we came to interesting (if far-stretched) conclusion.<br /> <br /> Masquerade adheres more to the European Gothic genre (and, ultimately, scene, intertwined with Punk elements that attest to the origin of Gothic music), with ancient cosmic horrors that still haunt the oldest, largest cities of Europe, while the inclusion of Northern America in the larger picture (aka the Jyhad) is still underway. Old rivalries and terrors dating back to the Crusades and the antiquity, still shape the nights tonight. Of course, many of those are not elements of Gothic literature, but they ultimately these tropes were tied to Gothic Horror and Gothic Punk (partly because of innovations introduced by Masquerade).<br /> <br /> Requiem, just based on the vibe we get from it, feels like Gothic Americana, fitting more accurately the definition of Southern Gothic we found on Wikipedia. The characters themselves are the source of horror, rather than the ancient forces that be like in VtM. Characters like the Unholy and the flavor of the Gangrel clanbook, as well as that Southern aristocratic vibe I get from the Invictus, seem to support this (?). In-game conflict and political history seems to resemble Old World baggage, more than an ever-present threat. Big power players of the Danse Macabre do not necessarily receive orders from ancients, but simply try to keep alive the <i>old ways</i>, those of their sires and grandsires.<br /> <br /> Edit: I'm not saying this in a bad way, I really <i>really</i> like the feel of Requiem.<br /> <br /> What do you think?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Gothic\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Marlon_Brando_and_Vivien_Leigh_in_A_Streetcar_Named_Desire.jpeg/1280px-Marlon_Brando_and_Vivien_Leigh_in_A_Streetcar_Named_Desire.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Gothic\" target=\"_blank\"> Southern Gothic - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Gothic\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Gothic </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 01-18-2017, 10:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T09:50:30-08:00","id":1034089,"title":"","content":"That's a really interesting way of looking at it. I have long said that Masquerade is more Punk, Requiem is more Goth, in reference to the music genres and the subcultures surrounding them. Both deal with existential angst, but the former externalizes it and the latter internalizes it. But comparing to literature, it makes a lot of sense to say that Masquerade is more European Gothic and Requiem is more Southern Gothic. Very cool analogy!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T09:59:49-08:00","id":1034095,"title":"","content":"in general chronicles of darknes fumigates all the camp that permeated CWOD<br /> <br /> Camp isnt always a bad thing or a good thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T10:03:34-08:00","id":1034096,"title":"","content":"I don't know jack about literature, but this sounds pretty convincing. I should go read the Wikipedia articles about Goth...-ism? Gothicness? Goths? Gothic literature. Yeah, that sounds good.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1034095#post1034095\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">in general chronicles of darknes fumigates all the camp that permeated CWOD<br /> <br /> Camp isnt always a bad thing or a good thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not really sure how that's relevant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/127887-doing-first-tongue-translations?p=484226#post484226\" target=\"_blank\">Nihhina kalekal-zidu kal masun, kal manudanadu. Nihhina kalekal-zidu nukal shaghu-desasudu \u2014 nihhina kalekal-zidu kal innu-desasudu udhkal samm</a>.&quot; Arthur Ashe<br /> She/her, contributing writer for <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/363404/Scion-Pride-2021\" target=\"_blank\">Scion: Pride 2021</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1923-bigdamnhero","name":"BigDamnHero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1923_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1263"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T12:30:58-08:00","id":1034171,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the Southern Gothic is a big inspiration for <i>Vampire</i>. Pretty sure I've even quoted Flannery O'Connor in a book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T18:22:01-08:00","id":1034374,"title":"","content":"While I find the comparison interesting (and I'm not going to contradict Rose jumping in and confirming some influence there), I have problem with how &quot;European Gothic&quot; is being defined here.<br /> <br /> There's nothing particularly European about the way VtM modifies the basic Gothic literary tropes. When I think about things like cosmic horror and ancient mystic conspiracies, I think about the &quot;Northern Horror&quot; that Southern Gothic is compared to. People like Lovecraft, and King.<br /> <br /> Maybe a better contrasting term for VtM would be &quot;Existentialist Gothic.&quot; Existentialist literature (which horror authors like Lovecraft and Poe are often included in) plays on the external fears of loss of control or insignificance in the face of an uncaring universe. Existentialist literature also shares a lot of thematic overlap with the punk styling of the WoD (I've seen people label Fight Club as an example of this, to which I think is a good case).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T19:08:39-08:00","id":1034401,"title":"","content":"I never thought of either game in terms of &quot;Gothic&quot;, but instead I think of them in terms of scale. Both are excellent at what they do, but they focus on different things. Masquerade's scale is immense. The world it literally ending, or on the verge of it. Elder vampires have powers capable of wiping out towns. If elder vampires are practically demigods, the Clan founders are ancient otherworldly beings of a similar nature with Lovecraftien horrors, capable of bending the world with their dreams whilst they sleep, let alone when they wake. Organizations cross the world with immediate agendas they work at a fervor pitch to achieve. Where as Requiem for me is local, personal, the world is still ending, but not the world at large, YOUR world is ending. it's not about power, although that's a part of it. It's about redefining yourself after this transformation. Figuring out how much of your life can be saved and how far your life extended. You don't care about the world, or some ages old organizations, you care about your friends, your family, the city you grew up in, or the one you find yourself in. The rest of the world can take a hike, because your world is large enough as is. But all that is just my thoughts on the matter, everyone views things differently, and that's for the best, variety is the spice of unlife after all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12860-your3rdshadow","name":"Your3rdShadow","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12860_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-19T10:10:41-08:00","id":1034687,"title":"","content":"I think the amount and type of gothic vary depending on where you look. Early <i>Requiem</i> deliberately uses a lot of abstractly gothic aesthetics, particularly in the art. That drops out over time in favor of the very contemporary look and feel. I like both styles, but most of my work is on the contemporary stuff.<br /> <br /> Talking about very general influences, punk lyrics were a big influence on my early <i>Vampire</i> prose.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T15:48:49-08:00","id":1037455,"title":"","content":"Surely I am not saying anything original, but these really interesting considerations take Requiem-Masquerade integration to a much more interesting level.<br /> Camarilla and Sabbat fighting in the Old World at the tune of Ancient Jyahd, facing millennia-old threats and myths, corporatist oppression against religious fanaticism.<br /> <br /> The New World, home of Ancillae and adventurers, jealous and loosely aligned Groups of vampires building their influence while exploring their damnation with lesser concerns due to vast distances.<br /> <br /> Mekhet and Daeva as New World bloodlines or multiclan strange groupings: the mysterious and shadowy former Aztecs and Maya vampires and the new, syncretic Creolo vampiric subculture.<br /> <br /> Striges, a old foe reawakened by ... Wars in the middle East? Bahari cultists ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","name":"Manfr","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"449"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-24T15:53:23-08:00","id":1037456,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manfr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1037455#post1037455\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Surely I am not saying anything original, but these really interesting considerations take Requiem-Masquerade integration to a much more interesting level.<br /> Camarilla and Sabbat fighting in the Old World at the tune of Ancient Jyahd, facing millennia-old threats and myths, corporatist oppression against religious fanaticism.<br /> <br /> The New World, home of Ancillae and adventurers, jealous and loosely aligned Groups of vampires building their influence while exploring their damnation with lesser concerns due to vast distances.<br /> <br /> Mekhet and Daeva as New World bloodlines or multiclan strange groupings: the mysterious and shadowy former Aztecs and Maya vampires and the new, syncretic Creolo vampiric subculture.<br /> <br /> Striges, a old foe reawakened by ... Wars in the middle East? Bahari cultists ?</div> </div> </div> </div>I do love me some Masquiem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T15:41:58-08:00","id":1045436,"title":"","content":"Coming a bit lately, but...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem#post1033969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We've been discussing the thematic differences between Requiem and Masquerade with my troupe lately, and we came to interesting (if far-stretched) conclusion.<br /> <br /> Masquerade adheres more to the European Gothic genre (and, ultimately, scene, intertwined with Punk elements that attest to the origin of Gothic music), with ancient cosmic horrors that still haunt the oldest, largest cities of Europe, while the inclusion of Northern America in the larger picture (aka the Jyhad) is still underway. Old rivalries and terrors dating back to the Crusades and the antiquity, still shape the nights tonight. Of course, many of those are not elements of Gothic literature, but they ultimately these tropes were tied to Gothic Horror and Gothic Punk (partly because of innovations introduced by Masquerade).<br /> <br /> Requiem, just based on the vibe we get from it, feels like Gothic Americana, fitting more accurately the definition of Southern Gothic we found on Wikipedia. The characters themselves are the source of horror, rather than the ancient forces that be like in VtM. Characters like the Unholy and the flavor of the Gangrel clanbook, as well as that Southern aristocratic vibe I get from the Invictus, seem to support this (?). In-game conflict and political history seems to resemble Old World baggage, more than an ever-present threat. Big power players of the Danse Macabre do not necessarily receive orders from ancients, but simply try to keep alive the <i>old ways</i>, those of their sires and grandsires.<br /> <br /> Edit: I'm not saying this in a bad way, I really <i>really</i> like the feel of Requiem.<br /> <br /> What do you think?<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Gothic\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Gothic</a></div> </div> </div> </div>Hmmm. I think I'm agreeing with it.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2\" data-vbnamecard=\"2\">Rose Bailey</a>'s additions are appreciated too, it's always good to see a developer's thoughts. For myself, I loved the distinctly gothic aesthetics of WoD and Requiem 1e more than 2e's contemporary-styled choices. The writing was superb in the latter, but for me, the former ones had a much stronger visual impact, more distinct &quot;vampire&quot; feel. The 2e book still says &quot;gothic&quot; on the cover, but when skimming through the book, the most gothic parts, IMO are the page and text-box frames which kept the wrought-iron looks. I might add it's just my personal preferences and I know others like the looks of the new book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T15:56:47-08:00","id":1045444,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1045436#post1045436\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Coming a bit lately, but...<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Hmmm. I think I'm agreeing with it.<br /> <br /> <span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span>'s additions are appreciated too, it's always good to see a developer's thoughts. For myself, I loved the distinctly gothic aesthetics of WoD and Requiem 1e more than 2e's contemporary-styled choices. The writing was superb in the latter, but for me, the former ones had a much stronger visual impact, more distinct &amp;quot;vampire&amp;quot; feel. The 2e book still says &amp;quot;gothic&amp;quot; on the cover, but when skimming through the book, the most gothic parts, IMO are the page and text-box frames which kept the wrought-iron looks. I might add it's just my personal preferences and I know others like the looks of the new book.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm... I'm not sure what you mean by &quot;Gothic&quot; that is present in 1e and absent from 2e...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T16:59:12-08:00","id":1045497,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1045444#post1045444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm... I'm not sure what you mean by &quot;Gothic&quot; that is present in 1e and absent from 2e...</div> </div> </div> </div>Largely the entirety of the visual style, as Rose mentioned earlier. The b&amp;w arts, the arranging of the scenes, the clothes, the backgrounds. The Bradstreet full-pagers. To me, it had a very distinct Vampire-feel, different from Masquerade, but still one of my favorite books visually. One of my best friends, who isn't an rpg-player, or WoD-fan (not even a goth, or metalhead, although he could appreciate &quot;dark&quot; things aesthetically), said when I shown him the 1e and 2e books, one after another, that when looking at just the pictures of the 1e book, skimming through, he immediately entered the state of mind akin to the one when watching for example Interview with the Vampire, while the 2e pictures are felt more like they could show anything, most of the time, like everyday scenes and people. Not all of them, but the overall feeling of the book was just less Vampire-ish. Of course, on the other end, one could say that the old books were caricaturistically goth-y (as the subculture) and vampire-y in a cheesy way. Different people, different tastes, as always.<br /> <br /> Edit: I'd even go further and say, that parts of the aesthetics that remained from 1e, like the aforementioned borders, or the symbols of clans and covenants feels to me a little out of place among the overall different visual style of 2e. For me, the two just doesn't sync, although it's highly personal, of course.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-07-2017, 08:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T10:50:05-08:00","id":1045861,"title":"","content":"Vampires are alternately pretentious and crass. They're the kind of people who put tidy little wrought-iron fences around everything... then proceed to splatter the rest of the page in blood.<br /> <br /> Another term I used a lot, back when we relaunched the line in 2007, was &quot;concrete gothic.&quot; Less gargoyles and corsets, more the romance of the city and the skyline looming like a predator.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T14:20:44-08:00","id":1045995,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rose Bailey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1045861#post1045861\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Vampires are alternately pretentious and crass. They're the kind of people who put tidy little wrought-iron fences around everything... then proceed to splatter the rest of the page in blood.</div> </div> </div> </div>Always liked the blood-spattered theme and the requiem wrought-iron borders nearly or just as much as Masquerade's green marble+rose+b&amp;w cemetery gate borders. Both are very strong visually IMO.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Another term I used a lot, back when we relaunched the line in 2007, was &quot;concrete gothic.&quot; Less gargoyles and corsets, more the romance of the city and the skyline looming like a predator. </div> </div> </div>Honestly, gargoyles and corsets might be the basic gothic rope, but I'm not sure that Req 1e, or even Masq had all that much of them. Ok, maybe little more corsets here and there, but I think it was more of a punk/fetish/high class direction as clothing on pictures went. The gothic aesthetics were more in the backgrounds and architecture on - and the overall arrangements of - the pictures I think.<br /> <br /> Your 'concrete gothic' is an interesting concept, although, in my opinion it's more like 2e kept the gothic themes and romance in the game itself and in the writing (I won't argue about that in the least). However, the illustrations themselves are indeed more like contemporary horror/everyday city than anything gothic. It just doesn't bleed the gothic feel as 1e and Masq did. I want to stress that I won't argue neither that It's an absolutely legit choice on your part as a developer to alter the visual style to your tastes and vision. Some people, like me might like it less, because in our minds the gothic aesthetics and vampires are something like hot-dog and mustard. Not in the sense of every vampire dressing as getting ready to a night at the Waydown (although the overall attire of charcers could be part of it, yes), but in the sense of general feeling and backgrounds.<br /> Of course I could imagine someone who doesn't like that much, thinking all those iron fences and spires and bare branches in front of the moon are painstakingly pretentious and groans every time something like that coming up in the illustrations, who want to see 'normal' people and scenes because it feels more realistic and/or easier to relate. I don't say either approach is objectively better. I just said my personal preferences and that in my opinion (and in the opinion of my friends/acquaintances) the gothic aesthetic conveys the vampire theme more strongly and added that also in my opinion, the more Southern Vampire feel* of the 2e illustrations are a bit out of sync with the visual bits kept from 1e, which were designed with the gothic aesthetic style in mind.<br /> <br /> To being constructive, one of the directions I think would be good for Requiem, if you want it truly distanced from the gothic looks is something akin to Only Lovers Left Alive (which is the most Requiem-ish movie for me, accidentally. Martin said it's the ultimate Masq 1e film, but I started with Revised, so it's a Requiem film for me as general feeling goes <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ).<br /> <br /> *Point of fact: I read all of the Southern Vampire series and liked it, in general.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-08-2017, 05:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T17:55:20-08:00","id":1046088,"title":"","content":"Ok, I have to say this: This thread need to have some serious clarification about what it means when it says, Just how &quot;Gothic&quot; is Requiem?<br /> <br /> Because: Gothic as a literary art form (because that is what I assumed it was asking since these are storyteller games after all) is a sub genre of Romantasism, and doesn't have any ties directly to the Cosmic Horror genre beyond what contemporary authors have ascribed to them. Nor does it have any direct connection to the Punk genre as well, because as far as I'm aware Punk is a sub genre of Expressionism (I very well might be wrong, but I'm quite sure it doesn't have it's roots in Romantasism or Gothic literature.)<br /> <br /> So, is &quot;Gothic&quot; really Gothic? Or is there a different question being asked here?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":"self checking own ego. 02-08-2017, 09:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T18:10:33-08:00","id":1046096,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dusksage</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046088#post1046088\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok, I tried but can't take it any more. This thread need to have some serious clarification about what it means when it says: Just how &quot;Gothic&quot; is Requiem?<br /> <br /> Because: Gothic as a literary art form (because that is what I assumed it was asking since these are storyteller games after all) is a sub genre of Romantasism, and doesn't have any ties directly to the Cosmic Horror genre beyond what contemporary authors have ascribed to them. Nor does it have any direct connection to the Punk genre as well, because as far as I'm aware Punk is a sub genre of Expressionism (I very well might be wrong, but I'm quite sure it doesn't have it's roots in Romantasism or Gothic literature.)</div> </div> </div> </div>It depends. &quot;Gothic&quot; could mean a number of things. It could mean the literary genre as you wrote, but it could mean also mean the musical and sub-cultural genre with all it's sub-categories, implications and trappings and it could also mean the architectural style and it's trappings. IMO usually all of them are intertwined in products, like WoD and CofD, because they influenced and built on each-other through history.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T18:16:42-08:00","id":1046099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1045995#post1045995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Always liked the blood-spattered theme and the requiem wrought-iron borders nearly or just as much as Masquerade's green marble+rose+b&amp;w cemetery gate borders. Both are very strong visually IMO.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Honestly, gargoyles and corsets might be the basic gothic rope, but I'm not sure that Req 1e, or even Masq had all that much of them. Ok, maybe little more corsets here and there, but I think it was more of a punk/fetish/high class direction as clothing on pictures went. The gothic aesthetics were more in the backgrounds and architecture on - and the overall arrangements of - the pictures I think.<br /> <br /> Your 'concrete gothic' is an interesting concept, although, in my opinion it's more like 2e kept the gothic themes and romance in the game itself and in the writing (I won't argue about that in the least). However, the illustrations themselves are indeed more like contemporary horror/everyday city than anything gothic. It just doesn't bleed the gothic feel as 1e and Masq did. I want to stress that I won't argue neither that It's an absolutely legit choice on your part as a developer to alter the visual style to your tastes and vision. Some people, like me might like it less, because in our minds the gothic aesthetics and vampires are something like hot-dog and mustard. Not in the sense of every vampire dressing as getting ready to a night at the Waydown (although the overall attire of charcers could be part of it, yes), but in the sense of general feeling and backgrounds.<br /> Of course I could imagine someone who doesn't like that much, thinking all those iron fences and spires and bare branches in front of the moon are painstakingly pretentious and groans every time something like that coming up in the illustrations, who want to see 'normal' people and scenes because it feels more realistic and/or easier to relate. I don't say either approach is objectively better. I just said my personal preferences and that in my opinion (and in the opinion of my friends/acquaintances) the gothic aesthetic conveys the vampire theme more strongly and added that also in my opinion, the more Southern Vampire feel* of the 2e illustrations are a bit out of sync with the visual bits kept from 1e, which were designed with the gothic aesthetic style in mind.<br /> <br /> To being constructive, one of the directions I think would be good for Requiem, if you want it truly distanced from the gothic looks is something akin to Only Lovers Left Alive (which is the most Requiem-ish movie for me, accidentally. Martin said it's the ultimate Masq 1e film, but I started with Revised, so it's a Requiem film for me as general feeling goes <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ).<br /> <br /> *Point of fact: I read all of the Southern Vampire series and liked it, in general.</div> </div> </div> </div>Im struggling to understand what qualitative distinction you're making in the art, apart from black-and-white vs. color. Other than being monochrome, what is it about 1e Requiem art that you find &quot;Gothic&quot; that is lacking in 2e art?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T18:56:34-08:00","id":1046115,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046099#post1046099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Im struggling to understand what qualitative distinction you're making in the art, apart from black-and-white vs. color. Other than being monochrome, what is it about 1e Requiem art that you find &quot;Gothic&quot; that is lacking in 2e art?</div> </div> </div> </div>Now, I'm not an art major, or anything, so I could convey my impressions only in a very vague sense and in layman's terms. Moreover I'm not sure you could get any qualitative answers. It's not like gothicness is measured in number of gargoyles. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Requiem 1e:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/images/1/1459.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.drivethrurpg.com/images/1/1459.jpg</a><br /> <a href=\"http://image.slidesharecdn.com/vampiretherequiem-140903180215-phpapp02/95/vampire-the-requiem-19-638.jpg?cb=1409767476\" target=\"_blank\">http://image.slidesharecdn.com/vampi...?cb=1409767476</a><br /> <a href=\"http://cdn.obsidianportal.com/assets/241669/bloodlines.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">http://cdn.obsidianportal.com/assets...bloodlines.jpg</a><br /> <br /> The monochrome style is part of it, yes, but it's more than just that. It's the arrangement of pictures, the characters showed, the ongoing action itself, the clothes, the backgrounds. The style of the drawings. The overall feel.<br /> <br /> While 2e:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://unpossiblejourneys.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/06/vampire-the-requiem-hero.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">http://unpossiblejourneys.com/wp-con...quiem-hero.jpg</a><br /> <a href=\"https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/736x/dd/a2/b0/dda2b0a81fe263d6e4974dcd042ebf15.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com...cd042ebf15.jpg</a><br /> <br /> I think the two has very distinctive differences in style and I associate &quot;gothic looks&quot; with the former.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T19:32:42-08:00","id":1046130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046115#post1046115\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, I'm not an art major, or anything, so I could convey my impressions only in a very vague sense and in layman's terms. Moreover I'm not sure you could get any qualitative answers. It's not like gothicness is measured in number of gargoyles. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well... Number of gargoyles would be quantitative, but point taken <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046115#post1046115\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Requiem 1e:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/images/1/1459.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.drivethrurpg.com/images/1/1459.jpg</a><br /> <a href=\"http://image.slidesharecdn.com/vampiretherequiem-140903180215-phpapp02/95/vampire-the-requiem-19-638.jpg?cb=1409767476\" target=\"_blank\">http://image.slidesharecdn.com/vampi...?cb=1409767476</a><br /> <a href=\"http://cdn.obsidianportal.com/assets/241669/bloodlines.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">http://cdn.obsidianportal.com/assets...bloodlines.jpg</a><br /> <br /> The monochrome style is part of it, yes, but it's more than just that. It's the arrangement of pictures, the characters showed, the ongoing action itself, the clothes, the backgrounds. The style of the drawings. The overall feel.<br /> <br /> While 2e:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://unpossiblejourneys.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/06/vampire-the-requiem-hero.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">http://unpossiblejourneys.com/wp-con...quiem-hero.jpg</a><br /> <a href=\"https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/736x/dd/a2/b0/dda2b0a81fe263d6e4974dcd042ebf15.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com...cd042ebf15.jpg</a><br /> <br /> I think the two has very distinctive differences in style and I associate &quot;gothic looks&quot; with the former.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm... Even with these examples I'm afraid I'm not seeing what it is you're alluding to. I mean, far be it from me to question your preferences. It is enough that you find the former more to your taste than the latter. I'm just not personally recognizing the distinction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T19:42:03-08:00","id":1046134,"title":"","content":"We can't really make statements about what art pieces are more and less gothic until we establish what our criteria and measurement for relative goth-ness is. I propose we use the International Kilo-bat Scale.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T19:55:35-08:00","id":1046141,"title":"","content":"I personally think the last link is the most gothic of the images due to the different motifs in it. But it's also a lot more colourful than the others with the grass and the faces being the warmest parts of the painting, which makes it in a way the most lively of the images. I believe that would clash against many common expectations since most gothic art uses bleak and/or limited colours.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T20:18:30-08:00","id":1046147,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046096#post1046096\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It depends. &quot;Gothic&quot; could mean a number of things. It could mean the literary genre as you wrote, but it could mean also mean the musical and sub-cultural genre with all it's sub-categories, implications and trappings and it could also mean the architectural style and it's trappings. IMO usually all of them are intertwined in products, like WoD and CofD, because they influenced and built on each-other through history.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> You see though, there's a danger in this. Becaus there is no hard metric for what &quot;Gothic&quot; is then the question in the post become something more closer to: Just how &quot;Delicious&quot; is Requiem?<br /> <br /> And the problem with that is it always boils down to personal taste in the end. And while there's nothing wrong with that, it kind of makes the header for this thread a bit misleading. Because there is a hard metric for the many forms of Gothic, as you said. Without a clear definition &quot;Gothic&quot; could mean practically anything, sometimes even many different things to many different people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":".  Polish 02-08-2017, 11:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T21:20:37-08:00","id":1046168,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dusksage</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046147#post1046147\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> You see though, there's a danger in this. Becaus there is no hard metric for what &quot;Gothic&quot; is then the question in the post become something more closer to: Just how &quot;Delicious&quot; is Requiem?<br /> <br /> And the problem with that is it always boils down to personal taste in the end. And while there's nothing wrong with that, it kind of makes the header for this thread a bit misleading. Because there is a hard metric for the many forms of Gothic, as you said. Without a clear definition &quot;Gothic&quot; could mean practically anything, sometimes even many different things to many different people.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course, the opening post <i>does</i> clearly indicate Stylanski's standard of Gothic. It quite clearly attributes the fear of old things (old cities, old rivalries, ancient cosmic horrors, the &quot;old world&quot; in contrast to North America, etc.) as the elements that define Masquerade's brand of Gothic. Granted, these seem more to me like elements of Lovecraftian literature than Gothic literature, but Stylanski did note that these are not elements of gothic literature but that they did contribute to Masquerade's particular brand of Gothic Punk. Likewise, Stylanski clearly defines the standard by which he is measuring Requiem's brand of Gothic by comparing it to American Gothic literature and Southern Gothic literature in particular, and further clarifies this by establishing that this type of horror arises from the characters rather than ancient external forces, and the old ways being baggage carried by the aristocracy as opposed to an ever-present threat.<br /> <br /> I mean, the thread title is unfortunately vague and somewhat bait-y, and the conversation has kind of drifted to the point where we're now talking about &quot;gothic&quot; in far more subjective terms. But the opening post was quite clear in what it meant by &quot;Gothic.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T22:53:58-08:00","id":1046191,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar269_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"269\" data-vbnamecard=\"269\">Charlaquin</a>, fair enough.<br /> <br /> I'm a bit of a stickler for terminology, and that might have lead myself to get just a tad bit carried away. :/","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-09T11:49:26-08:00","id":1046399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046168#post1046168\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, the opening post <i>does</i> clearly indicate Stylanski's standard of Gothic. It quite clearly attributes the fear of old things (old cities, old rivalries, ancient cosmic horrors, the &quot;old world&quot; in contrast to North America, etc.) as the elements that define Masquerade's brand of Gothic. Granted, these seem more to me like elements of Lovecraftian literature than Gothic literature, but Stylanski did note that these are not elements of gothic literature but that they did contribute to Masquerade's particular brand of Gothic Punk. Likewise, Stylanski clearly defines the standard by which he is measuring Requiem's brand of Gothic by comparing it to American Gothic literature and Southern Gothic literature in particular, and further clarifies this by establishing that this type of horror arises from the characters rather than ancient external forces, and the old ways being baggage carried by the aristocracy as opposed to an ever-present threat.<br /> <br /> I mean, the thread title is unfortunately vague and somewhat bait-y, and the conversation has kind of drifted to the point where we're now talking about &quot;gothic&quot; in far more subjective terms. But the opening post was quite clear in what it meant by &quot;Gothic.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Apologies for the title. I originally intended to also discuss Requiem's departure from the original art style and tone, which for some reason resonated as more &quot;gothic&quot;, but later decided to focus on the Southern vs. European bit.<br /> <br /> For what it's worth, going simply with the term &quot;gothic&quot; and its connotations, VtM 1e nails it. 2e felt like dark heroes (or urban dnd) and revised started the unfortunate era of WW designing <i>games</i> rather than worlds, atmospheres, moral dilemmas, and all the rest that made WW, well, WW (an era which ended as Requiem began setting the stage for 2e - RfR, clanbooks etc.).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 02-09-2017, 02:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-09T12:03:02-08:00","id":1046403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046399#post1046399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Apologies for the title. I originally intended to also discuss Requiem's departure from the original art style and tone, which for some reason resonated as more &amp;quot;gothic&amp;quot;, but later decided to focus on the Southern vs. European bit.</div> </div> </div> </div>No worries. If I had thought it was a problem I'd have asked you to change it. I think it's fine though, especially considering the actual content of the thread, which I think has been excellent.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046399#post1046399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For what it's worth, going simply with the term &amp;quot;gothic&amp;quot; and its connotations, VtM 1e nails it. 2e felt like dark heroes (or urban dnd) and revised started the unfortunate era of WW designing <i>games</i> rather than worlds, atmospheres, moral dilemmas, and all the rest that made WW, well, WW (an era which ended as Requiem began setting the stage for 2e - RfR, clanbooks etc.).</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. I'm not terribly familiar with Masquerade publication history, but I've been pretty clear elsewhere on how I feel about the &quot;designing games vs. designing worlds&quot; thing. Mainly that it's rubbish, though if one were to insist on drawing such a distinction I would certainly not describe the decision to make gameplay the higher priority as unfortunate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-09T14:28:03-08:00","id":1046481,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046403#post1046403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh. I'm not terribly familiar with Masquerade publication history, but I've been pretty clear elsewhere on how I feel about the &quot;designing games vs. designing worlds&quot; thing. Mainly that it's rubbish, though if one were to insist on drawing such a distinction I would certainly not describe the decision to make gameplay the higher priority as unfortunate.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's a matter of taste, I guess. I know some people <i>do</i> like DnD 4e, others dread it. As I'm writing this though, I just finished creating a character for a VtM 1e-only chronicle, so there's my two cents on my personal preference... :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-09T19:01:13-08:00","id":1046585,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046130#post1046130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well... Number of gargoyles would be quantitative, but point taken <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>You are quite right, forgive me, it was late here. I'm glad the overall meaning went through regardless. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hmm... Even with these examples I'm afraid I'm not seeing what it is you're alluding to. I mean, far be it from me to question your preferences. It is enough that you find the former more to your taste than the latter. I'm just not personally recognizing the distinction. </div> </div> </div>I think it's pretty safe to say that the two books have a very distinctively different visual approach, and honestly, I can't say more than Rose already did:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rose Bailey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1034687#post1034687\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the amount and type of gothic vary depending on where you look. Early <i>Requiem</i> deliberately uses a lot of abstractly gothic aesthetics, particularly in the art. That drops out over time in favor of the very contemporary look and feel. I like both styles, but most of my work is on the contemporary stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, even the lead developer says early (a question: where would you mark the point of change? I'm honestly curious, because I don't know the Requiem material as in-and-out) 1e played a lot more into the abstractly gothic aesthetisc visual style, than later books, and I presume, ultimately 2e, which favored a more contemporary look, I think it's suffice to say it is the situation indeed.<br /> <br /> Of course, a great test would be to show both books to a lot of people, rpg-ers, average pop-culture consumers, members of different subcultures, etc, and asking them which conveys a more &quot;gothic&quot; feel or which stress the notion of vampires and dark stories more? My example in my former post is just anecdotal, so it would be a very interesting endeavor.<br /> <br /> I'd stress again, it's not about which is better. I have my clear personal preferences, yes, which I won't pretend sanctimoniously aren't influenced by Masquerade and other '90's products of pop-culture. However, I could understand someone saying 2e is finally not like a goth coloring book ( which is a somewhat hilarious idea, how many shades of black are there?), that it was cheesy as a '90s comics, that the &quot;everyday&quot; looks of 2e is more relateable for more people and a number of other things. It's just as a valid opinion, IMO, as saying on my part that 1e had a more distinctly gothic and associated-with-vampires visual style and thus hammered down the visual impression more closely to what the game is about. It just conveyed the, what I deem as &quot;vampire feeling&quot; more strongly to me.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046134#post1046134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We can't really make statements about what art pieces are more and less gothic until we establish what our criteria and measurement for relative goth-ness is. I propose we use the International Kilo-bat Scale.</div> </div> </div> </div>That was a great pun! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The question itself is nonetheless interesting, I'll do some research into it!<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046141#post1046141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I personally think the last link is the most gothic of the images due to the different motifs in it. But it's also a lot more colourful than the others with the grass and the faces being the warmest parts of the painting, which makes it in a way the most lively of the images. I believe that would clash against many common expectations since most gothic art uses bleak and/or limited colours.</div> </div> </div> </div>I might say that picture is indeed one of my favorites from the 2e book, due to the more subtle use of gothic tropes and motifs. It's clever and unobtrusive. I wouldn't have chosen it as the literally starting of the book, I'd like a Bradstreet one, or something like those for that, but nonetheless it's a good picture. The bright overcast of the sky in a presumably night-time picture is quite odd, just as the warm skintone of the characters, but I could write the latter up as how 2e changed the looking-as-humans thing. With a more dark complexion (especially in b&amp;w <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ) I'd deem it as a great illustration.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046168#post1046168\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, the opening post <i>does</i> clearly indicate Stylanski's standard of Gothic. It quite clearly attributes the fear of old things (old cities, old rivalries, ancient cosmic horrors, the &quot;old world&quot; in contrast to North America, etc.) as the elements that define Masquerade's brand of Gothic. Granted, these seem more to me like elements of Lovecraftian literature than Gothic literature, but Stylanski did note that these are not elements of gothic literature but that they did contribute to Masquerade's particular brand of Gothic Punk. Likewise, Stylanski clearly defines the standard by which he is measuring Requiem's brand of Gothic by comparing it to American Gothic literature and Southern Gothic literature in particular, and further clarifies this by establishing that this type of horror arises from the characters rather than ancient external forces, and the old ways being baggage carried by the aristocracy as opposed to an ever-present threat.</div> </div> </div> </div>I liked the OP's line of thought too, it's an interesting one. The external/internal nature of horror reminded me to thinking about how the examples of external horror in literature or games are the manifestations of internal conflicts and real-world phenomena too.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046399#post1046399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For what it's worth, going simply with the term &quot;gothic&quot; and its connotations, VtM 1e nails it. 2e felt like dark heroes (or urban dnd) and revised started the unfortunate era of WW designing <i>games</i> rather than worlds, atmospheres, moral dilemmas, and all the rest that made WW, well, WW (an era which ended as Requiem began setting the stage for 2e - RfR, clanbooks etc.).</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. Interesting. I started with Revised and I never felt it as artificial. what elements conveyed the impression to you?<br /> <br /> As for the dark superheroes thing: WoD was always not just gothic, but gothic-punk, so to me the action-side and the themes felt natural, however I never played it in full DnD mode and always felt that the comparison is a little over the top, but it's always good to hear other's experiences. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T03:49:56-08:00","id":1046701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046585#post1046585\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh. Interesting. I started with Revised and I never felt it as artificial. what elements conveyed the impression to you?<br /> <br /> As for the dark superheroes thing: WoD was always not just gothic, but gothic-punk, so to me the action-side and the themes felt natural, however I never played it in full DnD mode and always felt that the comparison is a little over the top, but it's always good to hear other's experiences.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, Revised did not feel artificial. I was perhaps thinking of Revised as setting the stage for the Time of Judgement, fixing the extremes of 2nd edition, and preparing the ground for the transition to nWoD (now CofD). Right now, my train of thought is heavily influenced from reading VtM 1e core, and creating a 1e character. The book reads clean, elegant, creative, open-ended, and atmospheric. It hit all the right spots of teasing what lies deeper into the shadows of WoD, while maintaining a sense of dread and awe (for example, the Lasombra quote or how the Sabbat are presented). 2e and Revised set fire to the shadows, while Requiem 1e swapped tease and dread for vagueness.<br /> <br /> Heck, VtM 1e almost reads like a FATE-based game, going as far as encouraging custom clans and Thaumaturgical rituals and paths. It was a really, really pleasant experience, and something which I feel has eclipsed in later publications. It puts forth an offering which I feel is missing from what is currently on offer by RPG products. Elegant simplicity (and yes, I get that a lot of the rules were not fully fleshed-out, which caused arguments around some troupe tables, but that's why STs should hide rolls and details from players) that excites and terrifies. It just hits the right spot between detailed and open.<br /> <br /> We were done with character creation in half an hour (for a group of 3 players, one a complete newbie), and everyone was eager to start playing. It's been a while since I've seen this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T09:50:14-08:00","id":1046820,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046399#post1046399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> For what it's worth, going simply with the term &quot;gothic&quot; and its connotations, VtM 1e nails it. 2e felt like dark heroes (or urban dnd) and revised started the unfortunate era of WW designing <i>games</i> rather than worlds, atmospheres, moral dilemmas, and all the rest that made WW, well, WW (an era which ended as Requiem began setting the stage for 2e - RfR, clanbooks etc.).</div> </div> </div> </div>Its interesting you should say that because my first encounter with Masquerade was Revised and to me it feels the most atmospheric of the Masquerade editions. By the same token my initial reaction to Requiem was that it was 'too' atmospheric. All style, no substance. Part of this was because at that time there was literally only one book, but the promise of substance later didn't make up for the lack of it in the moment. In that respect it was really kind of unfair for me to compare Revised Masquerade to the lonely Requiem 1E corebook, as the former had the advantage of leaning on the work done by the prior editions, where Requiem by dint of its trying to cut a different path wasn't able to really do the same thing. <br /> <br /> I've heard some people criticize Revised as being too polished and overproduced compared to 1st and 2nd edition, and have heard similar criticism leveled at Requiem. I can understand how the earlier editions felt more underground and immediate and raw, but at the same time one of the things that really drew me into Masquerade through Revised was how slick and stylized it was. Maybe it was trying too hard to be cool, but in the end it succeeded.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T11:13:40-08:00","id":1046848,"title":"","content":"Conversely, I discovered the &quot;of Darkness&quot; games via Requiem in 2010, and I never found it lacking in either style or substance. Masquerade was its own separate world, walled off from my experience by an impenetrable barrier of lore and alternate history, while Requiem was a better designed game, set in a world that more closely reflected reality, with a rich backstory that left room for mystery and intrigue. All of the Masquerade fans who tried to convince me it was better relied on critiques of Requiem that were factually inaccurate and praise of aspects of Masquerade that were also present in Requiem.<br /> <br /> These kinds of evaluations are highly subjective and heavily impacted by the contexts in which we experienced the games. I much prefer analyzing their literary influences and approaches to horror than talking about waffle like world first vs game first and style vs substance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T17:22:26-08:00","id":1046981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046701#post1046701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">True, Revised did not feel artificial. I was perhaps thinking of Revised as setting the stage for the Time of Judgement, fixing the extremes of 2nd edition, and preparing the ground for the transition to nWoD (now CofD). Right now, my train of thought is heavily influenced from reading VtM 1e core, and creating a 1e character. The book reads clean, elegant, creative, open-ended, and atmospheric. It hit all the right spots of teasing what lies deeper into the shadows of WoD, while maintaining a sense of dread and awe (for example, the Lasombra quote or how the Sabbat are presented). 2e and Revised set fire to the shadows, while Requiem 1e swapped tease and dread for vagueness.<br /> <br /> Heck, VtM 1e almost reads like a FATE-based game, going as far as encouraging custom clans and Thaumaturgical rituals and paths. It was a really, really pleasant experience, and something which I feel has eclipsed in later publications. It puts forth an offering which I feel is missing from what is currently on offer by RPG products. Elegant simplicity (and yes, I get that a lot of the rules were not fully fleshed-out, which caused arguments around some troupe tables, but that's why STs should hide rolls and details from players) that excites and terrifies. It just hits the right spot between detailed and open.<br /> <br /> We were done with character creation in half an hour (for a group of 3 players, one a complete newbie), and everyone was eager to start playing. It's been a while since I've seen this.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for sharing that. As I said, Sadly I don't have any personal experiences with Masq 1e and my 2e ones are mostly novels and some supplements. I'm planning to get the books eventually, if for nothing else but collector reasons, but they are not as easy to get from antiquaries here in Hungary (read: not present at all) and I'm currently short on money, so it's not a priority. Nevertheless it's interesting to read someone's fresh impressions, who isn't a WoD old-timer! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I don't have much time to write now, but I currently wrote some wall of text on the WoD forum under the topic &quot;Ponderings&quot; exactly about the curse and blessing of both the &quot;closed&quot; setting approach and the open one. Nothing really new and world-shaking, just my mind wandered around those questions and it was a good exercise to write them down and they might be applicable here too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-10-2017, 08:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T17:33:59-08:00","id":1046988,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046820#post1046820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've heard some people criticize Revised as being too polished and overproduced compared to 1st and 2nd edition, and have heard similar criticism leveled at Requiem. I can understand how the earlier editions felt more underground and immediate and raw, but at the same time one of the things that really drew me into Masquerade through Revised was how slick and stylized it was. Maybe it was trying too hard to be cool, but in the end it succeeded.</div> </div> </div> </div>Me too. Revised pushed all the right buttons to get under my skin immediately. As for trying too hard, one person's f*cking cool is another person's cheesy.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T19:14:44-08:00","id":1047016,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1046848#post1046848\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Conversely, I discovered the &quot;of Darkness&quot; games via Requiem in 2010, and I never found it lacking in either style or substance. Masquerade was its own separate world, walled off from my experience by an impenetrable barrier of lore and alternate history, while Requiem was a better designed game, set in a world that more closely reflected reality, with a rich backstory that left room for mystery and intrigue. All of the Masquerade fans who tried to convince me it was better relied on critiques of Requiem that were factually inaccurate and praise of aspects of Masquerade that were also present in Requiem.<br /> <br /> These kinds of evaluations are highly subjective and heavily impacted by the contexts in which we experienced the games. I much prefer analyzing their literary influences and approaches to horror than talking about waffle like world first vs game first and style vs substance.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's still a lot of knee jerk reactions and received lore floating around. There have been exceptions, but most of the time whenever I've gotten Masquerade partisans to actually sit down and <i>play</i> Requiem they've come away with a much better opinion. Admittedly this is in part because I've used the toolkit approach of Requiem to tailor a version of it that I personally find appealing, and as a former Masquerade partisan myself that probably means a Requiem setting I'd devise would be inflected in ways that would make it accessible to the same. <br /> <br /> From my own perspective it was the clanbooks more than anything that finally won me over.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T19:22:47-08:00","id":1047021,"title":"","content":"As in 1990s eyeliner Gothic, or the literary movement off of Romanticism Gothic?<br /> <br /> Masquerade is more the former. Requiem is more the latter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T20:34:56-08:00","id":1047041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1047021#post1047021\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As in 1990s eyeliner Gothic, or the literary movement off of Romanticism Gothic?<br /> <br /> Masquerade is more the former. Requiem is more the latter.</div> </div> </div> </div>^ This. This a thousand times. <br /> <br /> Been trying to find a way to express this, but glamourweaver put it quite simply and beautifully. And I personally believe this is because Punk is inseparable from the Masquerade aesthetic. If all elements of Punk was removed then the artistic design would be something far similar to the artistic aesthetic of VtR 2E's Core Rulebook. (Albeit less red and more marble)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":".  Polish 02-10-2017, 11:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T22:27:52-08:00","id":1047070,"title":"","content":"I won't say it's that simple.<br /> <br /> Masquerade indeed played into the gothic (which is sprouted from punk, so yeah, punk is a part of it) subculture's trappings in a big way aesthetically, but it also used the gothic literature genre's themes and tools too (don't forget that the goth subculture, while it birthed from the punk, is still strongly based on the gothic literature's influences too, which shaped it's said trappings). It never was just eyeliner, trenchcoats and punkishness, but those were a part of it to various extents, yes and it created a very distinct feeling and aesthetics which I happen to like quite a lot.<br /> <br /> <br /> IMO, Requiem 1e downplayed a lot of the punk angle thematically (not totally, but it's far less preeminent), but kept it visually, as part of the goth aesthetics, while, as Rose wrote, after a time, and now in it's latest iteration during 2e it distanced itself even visually from that. It kept the gothic literature's themes all along, nevertheless.<br /> <br /> So, in my eyes it's just a more comp\u0151licated question than Masq='90s eyeliner gothic, Req=romanticist literature gothic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T22:52:28-08:00","id":1047080,"title":"","content":"If we're talking about subcultures, Masquerade straddles the line between Punk and Trad Goth, bleeding into Deathrock where Requiem is more Romantic Goth, and has kind of evolved into a touch of Corporate Goth in 2e.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-10T22:54:32-08:00","id":1047082,"title":"","content":"You make some excellent points PM\u00e1rk, but this is where my incredible fixation with terminology comes in. <br /> <br /> Gothic sprouts from Romanticism, not Punk.<br /> <br /> Goth sprouted from Gothic and Punk.<br /> <br /> Masquerade does have strong gothic themes and aesthetics. But it's Punk aesthetic is part of it's Goth aesthetic, not Gothic aesthetic. <br /> <br /> *pant* you see where this kind of terminology gets confusing?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":".  Polish 02-11-2017, 02:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T07:33:03-08:00","id":1047200,"title":"","content":"It gets even more confusing since lower-case gothic (adjective) can be applied both to the subculture and the literary genre.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T10:08:17-08:00","id":1047291,"title":"","content":"Plus the original post wasn't so much a declarative statement on the definition of Gothic, but 'the vibe' they get from it. Which is pretty subjective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T13:05:15-08:00","id":1047365,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dusksage</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1047082#post1047082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You make some excellent points PM\u00e1rk, but this is where my incredible fixation with terminology comes in.<br /> <br /> Gothic sprouts from Romanticism, not Punk.<br /> <br /> Goth sprouted from Gothic and Punk.<br /> <br /> Masquerade does have strong gothic themes and aesthetics. But it's Punk aesthetic is part of it's Goth aesthetic, not Gothic aesthetic.<br /> <br /> *pant* you see where this kind of terminology gets confusing?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I get the desire for using the right nomenclature, don't worry. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> But as Tessie said, it's muddled. English is not my native language and in Hungarian adjectives are often the same form as nouns. In the case of Goth and Gothic we tend to use &quot;g\u00f3t&quot; for both (also for the architectural period, etc.). Furthermore, every time I searched for keywords on the internet, I found Goth and Gothic used more or less interchangeably (like: <a href=\"http://www.gothicsubculture.com/origin.php\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.gothicsubculture.com/origin.php</a>).<br /> <br /> So, I'm aware of the distinction between the literal genre and the subculture, yes, but didn't make the clear separation of the adjectives based on the drop-letters and/or form.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T13:42:39-08:00","id":1047378,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1047080#post1047080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If we're talking about subcultures, Masquerade straddles the line between Punk and Trad Goth, bleeding into Deathrock where Requiem is more Romantic Goth, and has kind of evolved into a touch of Corporate Goth in 2e.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmmm. I'd say both Masq and early Req had most of them. Take the Revised corebook, for example, the Tremere and Ventrue pictures on the pages of their write-ups are more-or-less corporate goth, IMO. I think trad goth is more restricted to certain places in WoD in general for example to the Hollow Ones (but I might be wrong, I sadly didn't saw the 1e and 2e corebooks this far).<br /> <br /> I don't think Req 1e is more romantic goth, visually than Masq, honestly, I don't really see the difference between the two, Req 1e, especially the corebook kept the overall aesthetics and tropes (the artists and their styles are varied, naturally). Most of the pictures wouldn't have been out of place in a Masq book. <br /> <br /> I agree with the Req 2e-corporate goth association, with the caveat that the few pictures I'd deem &quot;goth&quot;, the clothing of the characters would be indeed closest to corporate goth (suits, dresses, customs), but it's a bit tricky, because that's the style least different from average contemporary upper-class/corporate clothing. However, if I'm willing to label the Tremere in revised as corp goth, I'm willing to do the same for certain pictures in Req 2e. Nevertheless, most of the pictures in it are simply contemporary (horror), in my opinion.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T14:34:23-08:00","id":1047396,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1047378#post1047378\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmmm. I'd say both Masq and early Req had most of them. Take the Revised corebook, for example, the Tremere and Ventrue pictures on the pages of their write-ups are more-or-less corporate goth, IMO. I think trad goth is more restricted to certain places in WoD in general for example to the Hollow Ones (but I might be wrong, I sadly didn't saw the 1e and 2e corebooks this far).<br /> <br /> I don't think Req 1e is more romantic goth, visually than Masq, honestly, I don't really see the difference between the two, Req 1e, especially the corebook kept the overall aesthetics and tropes (the artists and their styles are varied, naturally). Most of the pictures wouldn't have been out of place in a Masq book. <br /> <br /> I agree with the Req 2e-corporate goth association, with the caveat that the few pictures I'd deem &amp;quot;goth&amp;quot;, the clothing of the characters would be indeed closest to corporate goth (suits, dresses, customs), but it's a bit tricky, because that's the style least different from average contemporary upper-class/corporate clothing. However, if I'm willing to label the Tremere in revised as corp goth, I'm willing to do the same for certain pictures in Req 2e. Nevertheless, most of the pictures in it are simply contemporary (horror), in my opinion.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not talking about the fashion of the people depicted in the art, I'm talking about the theme and mood expressed in the writing. Masquerade has a lot of that rebellious, counterculture vibe of Punk and Traditional Goth, wile Requiem is more introspective and melancholy, like Romantic Goth. I say 2e Requiem has a touch of Corporate Goth mostly because I think the style and themes have matured a lot since 1e, and the Corporate Goth stereotype is that they're goths who &quot;grew up&quot; and &quot;got a real job&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T15:18:02-08:00","id":1047421,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1033969-just-how-gothic-is-requiem?p=1047396#post1047396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not talking about the fashion of the people depicted in the art, I'm talking about the theme and mood expressed in the writing. Masquerade has a lot of that rebellious, counterculture vibe of Punk and Traditional Goth, wile Requiem is more introspective and melancholy, like Romantic Goth. I say 2e Requiem has a touch of Corporate Goth mostly because I think the style and themes have matured a lot since 1e, and the Corporate Goth stereotype is that they're goths who &quot;grew up&quot; and &quot;got a real job&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, I buy those similes. Mostly correct I feel, when considering themes and writing. However I might add it's a scale in every three cases, since Masq contains a fair share of introspective and melancholy elements, if you'd like to go that way with the game and both editions of Req contains punk elements to various extents (in that context, I'd even argue that 2e is more punk, because it speaks more freely and in a breaking-of-taboos way about certain things, I think).<br /> <br /> Nevertheless, I agree with your conclusion, by and large. In my earlier post, I associated to the fashion styles of the subculture, because prior to that, the discussion went to somewhat that direction (I mean, speaking about the subculture) and the goth archetypes are <i>mostly</i> (but not entirely) defined by the music and fashion statements.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 02-11-2017, 06:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-11T15:51:27-08:00","id":1047431,"title":"","content":"I love the label of 'corporate goth' to Requiem, because I really do feel like Requiem washed off the 'punk' and kept the gothic. There are no Anarchs in Requiem - the Carthians are the closest thing. And the Carthians can be just as totalitarian as the Invictus. There's a strong feeling of 'status quo is survival' in Requiem, whereas in Masquerade the goal is to tear everything down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12981-romulusgloriosus","name":"RomulusGloriosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12981_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"342"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}